---
// Comparison page. Copy authored against briefs/maltipoo-vs-shih-tzu.json (2026-07-13).
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumb from '../components/Breadcrumb.astro';
import AnswerCapsule from '../components/AnswerCapsule.astro';
import CaptionedBlock from '../components/CaptionedBlock.astro';
import FactsCard from '../components/FactsCard.astro';
import FAQ from '../components/FAQ.astro';
import CtaClose from '../components/CtaClose.astro';
import { site, whatsappLink } from '../data/site';
import { webPageSchema, breadcrumbSchema, faqSchema } from '../lib/schema';

const title = 'Maltipoo vs Shih Tzu in Singapore Heat | Maltipoo.sg';
const description =
  'Maltipoo vs Shih Tzu for Singapore homes: the flat-face heat question, coat workloads, independence vs velcro devotion, and HDB status for both.';

const crumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', url: '/' },
  { name: 'Compare Breeds', url: '/compare-maltipoo' },
  { name: 'Maltipoo vs Shih Tzu', url: '/maltipoo-vs-shih-tzu' },
];

const faqItems = [
  {
    q: 'Is a Shih Tzu safe in Singapore heat?',
    a: 'Yes, with sensible care: early walks, shade, water, air-conditioned rest. Its flat face makes panting less efficient, so it needs more heat caution than a longer-nosed dog. [VERIFY brachycephalic heat guidance against a vet source] The Maltipoo’s longer muzzle copes more easily.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which handles being alone better?',
    a: 'The Shih Tzu, clearly. It is famously self-possessed and will nap through your absence with dignity. The Maltipoo is attachment-dependent and wants company through the day; an often-empty flat suits the Shih Tzu far better of the two.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which is easier to train?',
    a: 'The Maltipoo, thanks to the Poodle side; toilet training in particular lands faster. The Shih Tzu is intelligent but independent-minded, which reads as stubborn in week one.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Malshi vs Maltipoo: what is the difference?',
    a: 'A Maltese crossed with a Shih Tzu, a different mix from the Maltipoo (Maltese x Toy Poodle). As a cross of two HDB-listed breeds it also qualifies under item 62.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Are both HDB approved?',
    a: 'Yes: the Shih Tzu is named at item 48 of HDB’s approved list, and the Maltipoo qualifies through item 62.',
  },
];

const schemas = [
  webPageSchema({
    path: '/maltipoo-vs-shih-tzu',
    title,
    description,
    aboutName: 'Maltipoo vs Shih Tzu comparison',
    datePublished: '2026-07-13',
  }),
  breadcrumbSchema(crumbs),
  faqSchema(faqItems),
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} path="/maltipoo-vs-shih-tzu" schemas={schemas}>
  <section class="container-site pt-4">
    <div class="rounded-[var(--radius-panel)] bg-tint-200 px-6 py-10 md:px-12">
      <Breadcrumb items={crumbs} bare class="mb-5" />
      <p class="eyebrow mb-2">Comparison</p>
      <h1 class="text-[clamp(1.9rem,4vw,2.8rem)] mb-3 max-w-[24ch]">Maltipoo vs Shih Tzu</h1>
      <p class="text-ink-soft max-w-[52ch] mb-5">
        One of Singapore's long-time favourites against the apricot newcomer, starting with
        the question our climate makes unavoidable.
      </p>
      <a href={whatsappLink('Hi! Could you help me decide between a Maltipoo and a Shih Tzu?')} class="btn-cta">Ask which fits you</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <AnswerCapsule question="Maltipoo or Shih Tzu: what is the difference?">
    The difference between Maltipoo and Shih Tzu starts at the muzzle. A Shih Tzu is the regal, more independent classic: 4 to 7kg, a long double coat or a
    practical puppy cut, and a flat face that makes it more heat-sensitive in Singapore. A
    Maltipoo is lighter (2 to 4kg), longer-nosed, clingier and quicker to train. Both are
    HDB-listed; heat tolerance and independence are the honest deciders.
  </AnswerCapsule>

  <CaptionedBlock heading="The heat question first" id="heat">
    <p>
      In this climate the shih tzu vs maltipoo comparison starts at the muzzle. The Shih
      Tzu's flat face makes panting, its main cooling system, less efficient, so hot midday
      walks and stuffy rooms carry more risk and its exercise clock lives at dawn and dusk.
      [VERIFY brachycephalic heat guidance against a vet source before launch]
    </p>
    <p>
      The Maltipoo's longer Poodle-side muzzle cools more conventionally. It still prefers
      the shaded, early routine every small dog here does, but the margin for a delayed walk
      is wider.
    </p>
    <FactsCard
      slot="side"
      heading="Heat, honestly"
      items={[
        'Shih Tzu: flat-faced, stricter heat caution',
        'Maltipoo: longer muzzle, wider margin',
        'Both: walk early, shade, water, aircon rest',
      ]}
    />
  </CaptionedBlock>

  <CaptionedBlock heading="Temperament: regal vs velcro" id="temperament" reverse>
    <p>
      The Shih Tzu was bred to decorate palaces and knows it: affectionate on its own
      schedule, self-possessed, and genuinely fine napping through your workday. The Maltipoo
      is the opposite pole, the velcro dog of the
      <a href="/maltipoo-temperament">temperament guide</a>, devoted, vocal about loneliness,
      and happiest with company through the day.
    </p>
    <p>
      That makes this the rare comparison where the honest routing runs against us: if your
      flat is empty from 8am to 6pm, the Shih Tzu is the better-suited dog of these two, and
      we will say so.
    </p>
    <FactsCard
      slot="side"
      heading="Character split"
      items={[
        'Shih Tzu: independent, dignified, ok alone',
        'Maltipoo: velcro, vocal, needs company',
        'Training speed: Maltipoo clearly faster',
        'Empty weekday flat: Shih Tzu wins this one',
      ]}
    />
  </CaptionedBlock>

  <CaptionedBlock heading="Coat and grooming" id="coat" tinted>
    <p>
      The Shih Tzu's floor-length show coat is a daily project, which is why most Singapore
      Shih Tzus live in a standing puppy cut; budget the same four-to-six-week salon cadence
      either way. The Maltipoo's teddy coat runs the routine covered in the size and
      grooming guides: brushes between visits, mats punished by humidity, tear stains on
      light faces for both breeds.
    </p>
    <FactsCard
      slot="side"
      heading="Coat care"
      items={[
        'Shih Tzu: long coat or standing puppy cut',
        'Maltipoo: teddy cut, few brushes a week',
        'Both: 4 to 6 week salon cadence',
        'Both: tear stains on light faces',
      ]}
    />
  </CaptionedBlock>

  <CaptionedBlock heading="Who should pick which" id="verdict">
    <p>
      Pick the Shih Tzu if you want a composed classic that tolerates your absences and you
      can run a heat-smart routine. Pick the Maltipoo if someone is home most of the day and
      you want a lighter (see the <a href="/maltipoo-size">size guide</a>), faster-training
      shadow. On HDB paperwork they tie: item 48 for the Shih Tzu on the
      <a href="https://www.hdb.gov.sg/managing-my-home/home-ownership/keeping-pets" rel="noopener" target="_blank">approved list</a>,
      item 62 for the Maltipoo.
    </p>
    <FactsCard
      slot="side"
      heading="The one-line verdict"
      items={[
        'Out often, want composure: Shih Tzu',
        'Home often, want a shadow: Maltipoo',
        'Midday-walk-only households: neither, honestly',
      ]}
    />
  </CaptionedBlock>

  <FAQ items={faqItems} />

  <CtaClose
    secondaryHeading="Compare more breeds"
    secondaryText="Cavapoo, Maltese, Toy Poodle, Bichon and Cockapoo, all against the Maltipoo."
    secondaryHref="/compare-maltipoo"
    secondaryLabel="See all comparisons"
  />
</BaseLayout>
